{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-motif-review.md", "content": "# Noisy motif transformation review

For a sparse colored motif embedded in a noisy binary field, record the exact changed-cell mask for every demonstration before proposing a rule. Distinguish seed motif cells, random background coincidences, and newly transformed cells. Check all apparent translated motif placements and treat untransformed near-matches as negative evidence; do not infer template-copying merely because some scattered locations resemble the seed. Compare complete masks across demos, including preserved cells and dimensions.

After an incorrect submission, discard the complete mechanism rather than adjusting only a palette mapping, phase, or a few cells. Rebuild the candidate set from all demonstrations and choose only a materially distinct rule whose trigger, extent, and exceptions explain every observed change. If evidence remains underdetermined, be candid internally about that uncertainty and spend the remaining attempt on the most demo-consistent whole-grid prediction, not a sequence of cosmetic variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-motif-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/this-session-lessons.md", "content": "# Costly Continual-ARC action discipline

This session illustrates two recurring hazards in sparse-object transformations. First, a visually plausible operation on the test grid is not evidence: the initial submission added a color to the top row even though no demo supported that change. Second, after a rejection, a shifted copy of the motif is still a weak cosmetic variant unless a rule derived from the demonstrations predicts it.

Before acting, describe the mapping of all changed and unchanged demo cells and apply the candidate transformation to each full demo. In this case the demos are not enough to justify either the top-row insertion or simply translating the test motif upward; the second demo's input and output differ only by a one-row downward translation of the object, while the first pair has a particular top-row modification unrelated to a generic translation. Treat that discrepancy as a concrete unresolved rule question. Spend another demo request when it can discriminate between a context-triggered edit and object/position-based transformation, rather than guessing or making a cosmetic revision. After rejection, rebuild the hypothesis from the entire evidence set before spending another submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/this-session-lessons.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/multiple-outline-effects.md", "content": "# Distinguishing multiple outlined-object effects

When a scene contains several hollow or outlined motifs, create a per-object ledger: bounds, border color, interior pattern, embedded exceptional cells, and exact output changes. Infer the trigger independently from the operation and its extent. A task may combine independent effects—for example, filling the interior of one enclosure while changing an exceptional-color center cell in other motifs. Do not assume that every similar-looking enclosure receives the same operation or that all interiors should be filled globally.

Compare each object role across every demo, including unchanged objects and preserved borders. After a rejected output, discard the whole selection/operation hypothesis and audit every object rather than tweaking isolated cells. Request a demo only if competing object-selection rules predict different test outputs and the information justifies its cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/multiple-outline-effects.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/sparse-span-fill-and-validation.md", "content": "# Sparse marker span-filling audit

In uniform-background grids with sparse colored markers, avoid assuming that all cells between the global extrema in each row or column are recolored. Compare each demo cell-by-cell and distinguish uninterrupted marker-bounded spans from runs interrupted by markers and from background outside the endpoints. Test row-wise and column-wise span hypotheses separately; output may combine both orientations, and marker cells should remain unchanged if the demonstrations show that. Validate the complete predicted output against every demo before applying it to the test.

For programmatic construction, guard rows/columns with no markers before computing minimum and maximum coordinates. Finally assert the number of rows and each row width against expected dimensions; a malformed row must not be submitted.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/sparse-span-fill-and-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md", "content": "# Post-rejection extent and color audit

A binary wrong result rejects the complete proposed rule, not just a guessed cell. Before another request or submission, revisit all demos and partition the hypothesis into (1) trigger, (2) selected object or stroke extent, and (3) replacement color. Record exactly which cells change and which similar motifs remain untouched. Do not let a familiar visual analogy override these constraints.

For thin strokes among larger color masses, test the relation of each candidate stroke to mass boundaries and endpoints. A stroke color may be replaced by a mass color without changing the mass itself, while visually similar strokes can remain unchanged if the trigger is more specific. Verify each proposed trigger and transformed cell against every demo. A wrong first candidate is reason to rebuild the mapping, not to repeat the same theory or request another example without a concrete discriminating question.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md", "content": "# Rule audit after a rejected submission

A wrong submission rejects the whole hypothesis, not just one guessed cell. Re-derive from all demo pairs rather than iterating local edits. In cluttered inputs, identify regular object geometry and compare each output cell: isolated cells of other colors within otherwise uniform shapes may be noise or overlays, and the output may normalize the whole object while removing those intrusions. Conversely, do not generalize this without demo support. Audit both the transformed extent and every unusual cell outside it; test the candidate against every demo before spending another attempt.

When a submitted grid is not clearly supported, a further demo can be more valuable than another guess, but specify which competing interpretations it could distinguish. Keep strict protocol: exactly one JSON action per puzzle turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md", "content": "# Rejected global-fill hypothesis: preserve geometry, audit exact changes

In this Continual-ARC instance, several demos used noisy grids with one distinctive-color object. An attempted submission globally flood-filled every zero connected to the marker color, and binary feedback rejected it. The failure illustrates why connectivity alone is not sufficient evidence for recoloring: the demos preserve many background cells, and different examples show selective additions localized to patterned regions rather than blanket access from a seed.

After rejection, do not make a small arbitrary edit or repeat the same flood-fill with a different boundary. Reconstruct exact changed coordinates for every input/output pair, identify the marker object's shape and candidate repeated/translated structures, and check every unchanged background region. If the demos still support incompatible rules, request another only with a stated discriminating question; otherwise use a materially different candidate and validate it against the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/seed-expansion-phase-audit.md", "content": "# Seed expansion: phase and payload audit

For a lone non-background cell in a 1×N input that expands to N×N, derive the full output in stages rather than guessing a familiar template. Track each output color independently with exact row/column coordinates across every available size. Fit marker-generated arms, later periodic bands, transition row, phase, clipping, and overlap precedence; verify every demo cell before extrapolating. A pattern that matches the obvious diagonal phase may still fail because secondary-color onset or boundary behavior depends on N.

After a wrong expansion submission, reconsider the full mapping, not merely a phase shift. Distinguish semantic incorrectness from invalid-grid feedback: invalid shape requires mechanical checks that there are exactly N rows, each of width N, with values 0–9. This session's 1×13, 1×5, and 1×7 demos all expanded to squares with outward 2 diagonals and later 1 patterns; repeated width-17 candidates were rejected, so these examples did not establish a validated extrapolation. Treat that precise transition/phase as unresolved rather than canonizing a speculative template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/seed-expansion-phase-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/repeated-submission-evidence-reset.md", "content": "# Evidence reset after repeated wrong submissions

A binary rejection invalidates the full candidate rule, not a guessed subset of cells. Repeatedly submitting the unchanged test grid or cosmetic variants burns attempts without testing a new hypothesis. After the first rejection, reconstruct the transformation from all demos: exact changed-cell masks, preserved counterexamples, marker geometry, trigger, target extent, and color mapping. Before another submission, state internally what is materially different in the new rule and verify its predictions against every demo. If no candidate has passed that check, do not present an input-copy or lightly modified grid as a reasoned solution; use remaining actions only for genuinely revised hypotheses. This checkpoint applies especially when demo requests are exhausted.

Strict action-format compliance is necessary but not a substitute for evidence-led reasoning.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/repeated-submission-evidence-reset.md"}
